Design t_01a1d563324d7e8e §6/§9 (C1) names a host change-feed thread as the
gateway's phase-2 lever: about a dozen background loops each wake on their own
timer and probe or open their own inputs. This adds the feed itself.

- One daemon thread probes, once per second, every counter of the host:
  each local board's kanban_activity_revision content key (bookkeeping-only
  writes do not move it), hub.db meta.cursor (the seq of the last applied
  replica change; hub.db data_version also moves on hub bookkeeping) and
  state.db PRAGMA data_version from a retained read-only observer.
- Loops subscribe to topics (board:*, replica, state) and replace their sleep
  with Subscription.wait(timeout=<own next due time>) or wait_async(...).
  The wait returns the moved topics, or an empty set when the due time
  passed with no change, so each loop keeps its due-time wakeups and no
  recovery cadence is lost.
- Fail-open: an unreadable counter (or a failing probe) is UNKNOWN, which
  never equals anything, so its subscribers wake on every probe and run
  their own full pass. The first probe is only the baseline.
- Every probe is a change_feed.probe pass in loop accounting (P1).
- Under pytest the process feed never starts its thread.

Measured on hermes-hub against live data (12 topics: 10 boards, replica,
state), 120 probes: probe CPU median 1.33 ms, max 1.72 ms, i.e. 0.13% of a
core at 1 Hz. Over 120 s at 1 Hz, a board moved in 21 probes, the replica
cursor in 23, state.db in 59: a subscribed loop wakes on those instead of
on every timer tick.

Wiring is staged: the loops this feed is meant for (sync, notifier,
unloaded, remote index, links, change_watcher, session notifications) are
being rewritten right now by phase-1 cards F, H1, H2, H3 and J, which add
each loop's change key and due times. Each loop moves onto the feed after
its card lands, so the two changes do not collide.

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_host_change_feed.py (15). RED: before the
module existed the file failed at import; a mutant that drops _notify's
topics fails the 4 wake tests. Required cases: a subscriber is woken on
change (thread and asyncio), and on its due time with no change.
